d #uuid "032ef8bf-a149-5f5d-b9bc-54e6d8674bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.062" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345377} {:answer/id #uuid "3c56bb24-5e3e-5dba-9bd7-0f16e2f3edd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345861} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3bf950-dbc6-55c7-8257-4238683ae1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346447} {:answer/id #uuid "85c38cb7-115d-538e-baa8-9d152e8db340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346514} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf00326-ca9e-5146-a772-c3778f9f8641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3bf950-dbc6-55c7-8257-4238683ae1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346447} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c20e21-9157-5114-a78d-535633267fdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e921641-2f51-5af1-85b0-a27e011ac9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79836" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b7f183-ac89-5adb-9509-67e6410e2eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{d}r+40\\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375386} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9dd3ed-eaf8-543f-9cc9-8a1e7cc69c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+10\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375387} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad85924-7918-597c-a6e9-8076e5041dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{d}r+6\\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375388} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9e0aee-c171-578f-be62-2d9fa754d026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375389} {:answer/id #uuid "61520cb3-6fa1-591d-aec4-9e23c7e259a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61520cb3-6fa1-591d-aec4-9e23c7e259a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375390} :answer-field/id #uuid "894bb581-a8fd-5805-ac2d-24aef358fe0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddfbfeb1-47d1-5e4a-929c-ad72e52c8b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79959" :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the lateral surface area $S$ of the right cone if the base radius is $r=6,$ and the slant height is $l=10.$" :question/worked-solution "The  lateral surface area of the right cone is $S=\\pi rl.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(rl) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂S}{∂l})\\,\\text{d}l \\\\  & =\\pi l\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi r\\,\\text{d}l\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6,$ and $l=10,$  the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(6,10) & =10\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+6\\pi \\,\\text{d}l.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4664834b-f9c2-5711-9fbe-9e47c49b3446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352218} {:answer/id #uuid "57f10dd3-b6db-56b3-a0bc-7ae2adcbd893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352342} {:answer/id #uuid "589f8986-d7f3-5f81-95dc-09885e486e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352346} {:answer/id #uuid "acef1955-0448-5736-b63d-c7e469566b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353034} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ea3e5d-e86e-5f69-be8a-a181e3697068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acef1955-0448-5736-b63d-c7e469566b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353034} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e0aaa07-d114-533e-b9c9-fc1efde6d6b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed7f3fdb-ae1d-565d-b08f-f4ca7b1197c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86716" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=(2x+{y}^{2}-z)^{2},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1.5,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}x+4y(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}y-2(2x+{y}^{2}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,2,0) & =4(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}x+(4\\cdot 2)(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y-2(2\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{d}x+48\\,\\text{d}y-12\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,2+Δy,0+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈24\\,Δx+48\\,Δy-12\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1.5,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-2=-0.5 \\\\ Δz & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈24\\,Δx+48\\,Δy-12\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈24\\cdot 1+48\\cdot (-0.5)-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-12.$"}]}
